fa)\" style=\"fill: #c2410c\"/></g><g id=\"patch_5\"><path d=\"M 40.7 133.76 L 40.7 11.792 \" style=\"fill: none; stroke: #c9d1dc; stroke-width: 0.8; stroke-linejoin: miter; stroke-linecap: square\"/></g><g id=\"patch_6\"><path d=\"M 40.7 133.76 L 386.66 133.76 \" style=\"fill: none; stroke: #c9d1dc; stroke-width: 0.8; stroke-linejoin: miter; stroke-linecap: square\"/></g><g id=\"text_10\"><g style=\"fill: #2d3748\" transform=\"translate(120.428239 54.12) scale(0.08 -0.08)\"><defs><path id=\"IBMPlexSans-Regular-41\" d=\"M 1696 0 L 1120 0 L 2829 4006 L 922 4006 L 922 3174 L 442 3174 L 442 4467 L 3373 4467 L 3373 3994 L 1696 0 z\n\" transform=\"scale(0.015625)\"/><path id=\"IBMPlexSans-Regular-5a\" d=\"M 870 -77 Q 672 -77 576 28 Q 480 134 480 294 L 480 378 Q 480 544 576 646 Q 672 749 870 749 Q 1075 749 1168 646 Q 1261 544 1261 378 L 1261 294 Q 1261 134 1168 28 Q 1075 -77 870 -77 z\n\" transform=\"scale(0.015625)\"/></defs><use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-41\"/><use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-5a\" transform=\"translate(60 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-3c\" transform=\"translate(87.203125 0)\"/></g></g><g id=\"text_11\"><g style=\"fill: #2d3748\" transform=\"translate(292.755511 15.4) scale(0.08 -0.08)\"><use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-3b\"/><use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-38\" transform=\"translate(60 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-5a\" transform=\"translate(120 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-42\" transform=\"translate(147.203125 0)\"/></g></g></g></g><defs><clipPath id=\"p3d6be696fa\"><rect x=\"40.7\" y=\"11.792\" width=\"345.96\" height=\"121.968\"/></clipPath></defs></svg>\n","title":"Exhibit 1 · New trips a year (millions) for $30M","note":null},"answer":"<p><b>Free fares:</b> 60M × 12% = 7.2M new trips for $30M: <b>$4.17 per new trip</b>. And the $30M mostly subsidises the 60M trips already made.</p> <p><b>More frequent buses:</b> 60M × 18% = 10.8M new trips: <b>$2.78 per new trip</b>.</p>"},{"q":"Does it matter where the new riders come from?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<p>Yes: the goal is fewer car trips. Evidence from cities with free fares is that many new trips replace walking and cycling. Frequency improvements mostly win people who found the bus too slow or unreliable, more of whom were driving. So the gap in car trips removed is likely even larger than the gap in new trips.</p>"},{"q":"Free fares are popular and help low-income riders. How would you address that?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<p>Offer free or half-price fares only to low-income riders (about 35%), through existing benefit cards: half-price for them costs about $5.2M plus about $2M to administer, well under $30M. Combine that with frequency increases funded by the rest, if the budget allows.</p>"}],"reco":"Spend the $30M on more frequent buses, not free fares. More frequent service would add about 10.8M trips a year at about $2.78 each, against 7.2M at $4.17 for free fares, and more of those new riders would otherwise drive. To help low-income riders, offer targeted discounts through benefit cards for a fraction of the cost. Risk: frequency gains take time to show, so publicise the new timetable. Next step: model frequency increases route by route.","great":["Compares cost per new trip, not total riders","Asks where new riders come from (the real goal)","Keeps the equity aim with a cheaper targeted option","Phrases the answer for a political audience"],"id":93,"slug":"kestrel-metro"}